  "pattern": "arrays-hashing / Bit manipulation",
  "statement": "# Counting Bits\n\nGiven an integer `n`, compute and return an array `ans` of length `n + 1` such that for every integer `i` in the range `0 <= i <= n`, `ans[i]` represents the total number of `1` bits in the binary representation of `i`.\n\nConstraints: `0 <= n <= 500`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "n = 0",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "0 has no 1-bits in its binary representation, so the result is a single-element array [0]."
    },
    {
      "input": "n = 12",
      "output": "[0, 1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2]",
      "explanation": "For n=12, each entry ans[i] is the popcount of i. For example, 5 = 0b101 has two 1-bits, and 12 = 0b1100 also has two 1-bits."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function countBits(n: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Consider the binary representation of i and its relationship to i >> 1 (i without its last bit).",
    "The number of 1-bits in i is the popcount of i >> 1 plus the least significant bit of i (i & 1).",
    "Pre-fill ans[0] = 0, then for each i from 1 to n set ans[i] = ans[i >> 1] + (i & 1) in a single left-to-right pass."
  ],
